# `reverse` on a value whose type is not statically known.
#
# Unlike `length`, `isEmpty` and `contains` — where the String and List
# implementations happen to coincide in JavaScript, Ruby and Python — reversing
# a string and reversing a list are different operations in those targets, so
# the dynamic implementation has to work for both. JavaScript picked the String
# one and `reverse` of a list raised `split is not a function`.
#
# SQL is absent on purpose: REVERSE(text) and an ordinality subquery over an
# array cannot be the same expression, and PostgreSQL type-checks both arms of
# a CASE even when only one can run. On SQL `reverse` needs a known type.

assert(reverse(first(['abc'])) == 'cba')
assert(reverse(first([[1, 2, 3]])) == [3, 2, 1])
assert(reverse(first([''])) == '')
assert(reverse(first([[]])) == [])

# The typed twins
assert(reverse('abc') == 'cba')
assert(reverse([1, 2, 3]) == [3, 2, 1])
